1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,630
1,000,000 numbers.

2
00:00:01,630 --> 00:00:02,280
One target.

3
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:04,120
I'll check every single one!

4
00:00:04,380 --> 00:00:06,000
That's up to 1,000,000 checks.

5
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:07,340
Are you searching…

6
00:00:07,340 --> 00:00:09,210
…or taking a census?

7
00:00:10,020 --> 00:00:11,030
Fine, professor.

8
00:00:11,030 --> 00:00:11,720
What's your genius plan?

9
00:00:12,720 --> 00:00:14,670
If the list is ALREADY SORTED,

10
00:00:14,670 --> 00:00:16,670
binary search checks the middle…

11
00:00:16,670 --> 00:00:18,840
…then eliminates half the possibilities.

12
00:00:18,940 --> 00:00:19,670
Half?

13
00:00:19,670 --> 00:00:21,380
That's my motivation after lunch.

14
00:00:21,720 --> 00:00:23,280
1,000,000 items.

15
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:24,450
At most…

16
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:26,090
20 search steps.

17
00:00:26,970 --> 00:00:28,270
20?!

18
00:00:28,270 --> 00:00:29,790
I haven't even opened my code editor.

19
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:31,490
Sorted list, though.

20
00:00:31,490 --> 00:00:33,160
Sorting it first has a cost.

21
00:00:33,260 --> 00:00:34,470
So the winner is…

22
00:00:34,470 --> 00:00:35,820
…whoever read the requirements.

23
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:37,440
Finally.

24
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:38,660
A correct answer.

